WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our next guest is
an Oscar-nominated

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.870
and Emmy Award-winning producer
behind movies

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.173
such as "The Purge,"
"Us," and "Get Out."

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.109
His annual event, BlumFest,
featuring conversations with

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:12.111
Blumhouse filmmakers and actors,
is going on now.

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:14.280
And his latest movie,
"Halloween Kills,"

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.249
is in theaters
and streaming on Peacock.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:17.516
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:18.852
-What are you guys
doing out here?

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.219
-It's Halloween.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:21.654
We went trick-or-treating.
We got a whole bag of candy.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.322
-You guys should not be out here
right now.

00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:24.757
Okay? It's not safe.
-Ooh, I'm so scared.

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:25.691
-Are you guys alone?

00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:26.926
Where are your parents?
-No.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.427
-No, we're waiting
for our friend.

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:31.063
-And, like, there's a creepy man
in a white mask.

00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:33.399
And he keeps like trying to play
hide and seek with us.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:34.567
-Where did you see him?!

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:35.935
-He's just hiding behind trees.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.337
-And he pops out like,
"peekaboo."

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.738
I mean, we're not
three years old.

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:40.406
Come on, man!
-Oh, look, there he is.

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:41.908
-Oh, hello!

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:48.013
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:50.149
-Is that Dennis' mask?

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:52.018
-Run! Go home now!

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:55.388
-Please welcome to the show
Jason Blum, everybody!

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:57.657
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:03.729 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:09.802 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.571
Hi, buddy.
It's so great to have you here.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.840
-Well, it's --
I'm very excited to be here.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.975
Very excited. This is amazing.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:17.977
I've been dying
to get on this show.

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.912
-Well, this is big.
This is big for both of us.

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:21.647
-This is big.
-Congratulations.

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:23.015
This movie's doing great.

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:24.951
You obviously have been in
the horror business for a while.

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:27.420
I would imagine when you have
something like "Halloween,"

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.921
that people have
a relationship with,

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.991
you're making a sequel to it,
how much do you have to come up

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:35.461
with new and fascinating, and
dare I say, fun ways for people

00:01:35.461 --> 00:01:37.029
to get murdered?
-Well, you have to --

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:39.265
I mean, it's a big --
We have a science lab.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.468
We have to do
all these different things.

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:44.036
No, Danny McBride
and David Gordon Green

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:45.505
came up with these crazy ideas.

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:49.508
Someone gets -- One of the most
wild kills we've ever had

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:51.244
in a movie is they take
a fluorescent light bulb,

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.213
break it in half
and stab someone in the armpit.

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:55.114
-Yeah.
-And that was pretty amazing.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:56.615
[ Laughter ]
But, you know.

00:01:56.615 --> 00:01:58.284
-When they call you up
and tell you that,

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:01.955
are you just like mwah?
-I'm like, "Yes! Yes!"

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.691
And then they have to -- You
know, people count the kills.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.526
They're like,
"33 kills in this movie."

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.161
There will be 47 kills
in the next movie

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:09.295
or something, I don't know.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.331
-Was Halloween always
a big deal to you?

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:15.634
-The holiday of Halloween.
The movie not till I was older.

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:17.070
But the holiday of Halloween
was a big --

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.771
My mother and I loved Halloween.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:23.876
My mother and I would start
doing our costumes in August.

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:26.345
And I was a dinosaur once.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:27.880
I was the Abominable Snowman.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.818
Halloween was a huge, huge event
in our household.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:35.154
People always say, "Did you love
horror movies as a kid?"

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:37.223
Actually, I didn't.
I loved all kinds of movies.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.191
But I was a very odd child.
-Okay.

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:42.962
-And although this is
not Halloween-related,

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:46.366
one of the things that I did
when I was very young is

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.201
I collected my fingernails.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:50.370
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.204
-That is -- That is very odd.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:53.472
-It was very strange.

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:55.040
And somehow I think
this was a --

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.609
-You would clip them
and save them?

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.878
-They were a part of my body.
And I would --

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.146
-I think we know.
You can jump ahead.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.347
We know what fingernails are.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.383
-Sorry, yes. My mistake.
I'm very nervous.

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:06.652
-You're starting way --
-Yeah, too early.

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:09.488
Starting too early. Okay, yeah.

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:10.990
No, but I felt
they were part of me

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.291
and I didn't want to
let them go.

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:16.061
So I put them in a little
plastic box, which I still have.

00:03:16.061 --> 00:03:17.396
Now, one time --
-Wait.

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:21.033
-Hold on. It gets worse.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:25.171
One time, I actually -- This is
part of what was in the box.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.940
One time I grabbed a couple of
my mother's fingernails.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.542
[ Audience groans ]
So, in this box, there are

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.343
a bunch of tiny fingernails
from a little boy

00:03:31.343 --> 00:03:34.246
and about eight colored
fingernails from my mother,

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:35.581
sitting in this box.

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:38.117
This box has been with me
since I was five years old.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.353
I brought it to all the
different places that I lived.

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:45.391
And I very -- I thought,
astutely, hid this box

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.460
under my sink in my bathroom.
-Okay.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:51.664
-My wife, Lauren Blum,
used to be my fiancée

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.466
before she was my wife, before
that, she was my girlfriend.

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:55.601
When she was my girlfriend --
-We all know how that works.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:56.602
-Got it. Okay, thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:58.504
Thank you, thank you. Got it.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.206
She found the fingernails.

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:02.041
-No!
-Yes. And I still married her.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.542
That's how I knew
she was right for me.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:04.711
-Right.
-Yes.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.846
-I think I should stress
she still married you.

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:08.981
-That's what I meant!
That's what I meant.

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:10.649
That's what I meant.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:10.649 --> 00:04:12.284
-You were like,
"I'm still going to marry you

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.454
even though you snoop around!"

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:17.189
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:19.491
"You go where you're not
supposed to go, Lauren."

00:04:19.491 --> 00:04:20.826
-Correct.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:25.197
-One of my favorite things
when I first met you is,

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:28.334
obviously -- and I should note,
I think the first movie

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.236
that sort of brought Blumhouse
to prominence was

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.570
"Paranormal Activity."
-Correct.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.940
-So, this is a movie that
everybody had sort of passed on.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.675
What was it that you saw in it
that you thought,

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:38.478
"Oh, this is -- I actually think
an audience will love this"?

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:40.079
Which obviously
they ended up doing.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:42.181
-A long time ago
I worked for a company

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.016
and I passed on
"The Blair Witch Project."

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.185
-Wow.
-And I never -- I never --

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:48.788
You know, I was always punished
for passing on that movie.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.823
Everyone made fun.
Found footage, da-da-da.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.592
And "Paranormal Activity,"
when it first came out,

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:55.127
was about 10 years
after "Blair Witch."

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:57.030
So that experience
was relatively new.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.366
And I just saw it and then I
screened it with an audience.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.369
And anyone could have told you,
if they bothered to watch it

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:04.804
with an audience,
the movie was going to work.

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:08.140
But everyone had seen it on DVD
or on cassette and passed.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:12.477
It was going to home-video land,
and I said to the director,

00:05:12.477 --> 00:05:14.080
"I really think I can get this
into a movie theater.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.415
I passed on 'Blair Witch.'
I see the same thing here."

00:05:16.415 --> 00:05:17.717
It took three years to do,

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.852
but it really was
the birth of our company.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:22.721
So I'm very -- very --
It's a very beloved movie to me.

00:05:22.721 --> 00:05:24.990
-And you had a dream that
if you made money on a movie,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.293
you would get the car
that you'd always dreamed of

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.028
having to go to work in.
-Correct.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:30.429
-And I'm going to show
everybody.

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:32.231
I want everybody to imagine what
a big Hollywood producer wanted.

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.465
-This was my dream.

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:35.001
-And now I'm gonna show
everybody what it is.

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:37.770
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.973
I should note it says "Blumhouse
Electric" on the side of it.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:42.641
-And here, I don't know
if you can see,

00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:44.210
it says
"residential and commercial."

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:45.444
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:47.446
-But now, you -- This is
actually a functional van.

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:48.814
And the reason
you wanted a van --

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:52.384
-So, I've always been --
I've always lived in Los Angeles

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:53.953
but had been trying to move back
to New York.

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:56.422
It's only been about 22 years.
I'm still doing it.

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:58.190
And I can't -- One of the things
I don't like

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.558
about Los Angeles
is the traffic.

00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:00.693
And it's such a waste of time.

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:02.394
I'm a very, very
efficient person.

00:06:02.394 --> 00:06:04.263
So I said if I ever
have a hit movie,

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:06.665
I'm going to get a minivan,
hence the Chevy Astro,

00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:08.835
which is a very unusual minivan

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:11.270
'cause almost no minivans
have a flat back.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.872
They always have bumps
for the axle.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:15.741
The reason that handymen
always drive Chevy Astros

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:17.576
is because the bottom is flat.

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:20.947
I bought a minivan -- I bought
a used Chevy Astro for 10 grand

00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:23.249
and I paid a stereo guy $25,000.

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:24.550
He ripped out the back.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.320
He put in a comfy chair,
a computer, a printer,

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.955
when you used to have to
print boarding passes.

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:30.556
I used to print boarding passes
in that.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.957
[ Laughter ]
I still have a van.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:33.459
It's a little more upgraded
from that,

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:36.429
but it's my efficient way
to tackle

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:38.230
and never think about
Los Angeles traffic.

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.132
-It's really nice
that a kid creepy enough

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:42.300
to save fingernails ended up
in a white van.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.136
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.604
Jason Blum, everybody.

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:47.272
"Halloween Kills"
is in theaters now.

00:06:47.272 --> 00:06:50.076
For more info on BlumFest 2021,
go to blumhouse.com.

